hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in the  meditations as well as in other works  you're going to see Rene Descartes  making almost constant use of two terms  that play an incredibly important role  within his epistemology and his  reconstruction of the possibility of  knowledge those two terms are clear and  distinct or clarity and distinction and  he sometimes uses synonyms for these as  well if you're reading between the lines  so to speak you'll see many more  references and uses of these than simply  those two terms in meditation - we're  going to see it already being used quite  a bit and being invoked as an important  way of making sense out of our thoughts  and that a little bit later in  meditation 3 Descartes will actually  give us a rule that he's going to use  throughout the rest of the meditations  that anything that I conceive of clearly  and distinctly I can hold to be true he  doesn't quite have that working  explicitly in meditation - but he's  already making use of something like  and there's a lot of different ways in  which this comes up you know we can have  the general formula of conceiving ideas  clearly and distinctly what is it that  we're doing we're conceiving or thinking  or understanding or perceiving all of  these are modes of grasping something  and then what are we grasping in general  these can be called ideas or notions  right there are things that are present  to the mind and clarity and distinction  then are certain qualities or attributes  of those particular ideas or the way in  which we are grasping those ideas so  what is it then for things to be clear  and distinct this is something that  Descartes spends very little time  actually explaining he never provides a  formulation of precisely what it is for  an idea to be clear in the meditations  nor as a matter of fact in his discourse  on method instead he it's going to be in  his principles of philosophy that will  find this formulation of the idea being  present and accessible to the mind when  the mind is actually attending to it so  you know the opposite of clear is  obscure and a clear notion is something  which impresses itself upon us it has a  certain you could say self evidence in  how it is given to us so that we can say  ah yes I do in fact grasp what this  thing is so putting that aside saying  that we do understand what clarity is  because Descartes assumes that we we  really do have some sort of conception  of this we can say what's clear and  what's not clear what about distinction  this is a little bit easier to say  because Descartes is who's upon here  very clear about what it means to be the  state and for ideas to be distinct means  that there  not confused with other ideas we're able  to separate them out and see how they're  different from each other so Descartes  will use this language really throughout  meditation too and we can see a number  of different examples of this some of  which I want to focus on and one of the  most interesting ones is his famous  realization that if I am being deceived  if I am having any sort of thought  whatsoever then I must be able to say I  am I exist and this is often framed in  terms of what we call the coachee toe I  think therefore I exist this is  something that Descartes takes to be  completely clear and we can also say  that it's distinct from other  formulations other realizations the fact  of my own existence my knowledge or  notion of my existence is something  different from my notion of other things  existing and so this is something that  qualifies as very clear and distinct but  he talks about a lot of other things as  well as having a certain kind of clarity  and distinction for example a little bit  after that when he is talking about the  notion of body and he says that it has  all of these different characteristics  these are things that I can distinguish  apart from each other for example being  able to be in a space and exclude other  things from the space being able to be  seen heard sensed in other ways having a  certain extension or a figure whatever  we want to call it  all of those are distinct from each  other and those are also clear within  the conception of a body or a corporeal  thing or extended thing so he goes on  and he let's come back to the I am I  just the thinking thing he says that if  I think about what it means to be a  thinking thing a thing that thinks what  is that he says it's a thing that doubts  that conceives that affirms that denies  that wills that wills against that  imagines also and which senses or  perceives through senses and he says all  of these are distinct from each other  all of these are modes of thought that  we can distinguish and he knows clearly  or he has a clear conception of the fact  that he is doing those sorts of things  there's another passage a little bit  later he says it is of itself so evident  so clear we might say that it is me who  doubts who understands and who desires  that there is no need of anything else  to add in order to explain it so that's  another great example of something  that's a very clear set of ideas I know  that I am the one who desires I am the  one who senses I am the one who  understands and he says and it's also  certainly the power of imagining right  for just as it happens that that I  imagine the things the things that I  imagined are not true nevertheless this  power of imagining does not leave or it  is not a lack you could say any any  reality in me and and it's part of my  thought the things that I imagine I can  imagine in some cases at least quite  clearly and I know clearly that I have a  power of imagination so all of these are  presenting themselves with a sort of  self evidence you could say all of the  different modes of thought that are part  of the Cartesian coach ito that's  something we can be quite clear about  what about other things we can be clear  about the general notion of what  includes right the ideas of bodily  external things in general although he  says you know at some point sometimes  general notions are kind of confused  right and that would be the opposite of  being distinct but he says well if we  take individual things and here you  might think of the famous example of the  piece of wax or of other things that he  says could be equally applicable any  other physical thing including the  people outside of my window who I think  our people even though they could just  be machines or things like that insofar  as we're thinking about the thing in  terms of its substance that turns out to  be something that is quite clear and he  also goes on and he tells us that you  know when it comes to the sensations  that we have of it those sorts of things  are also clear and distinct ideas not  clear and distinct ideas in the sense  that they automatically tell us that the  thing that they're attached to or  signifying or whatever we want to say  they're representing it does actually  exist could be that no corporeal objects  actually do exist at this point but the  ideas that we have of them are at least  clear and distinct right he goes on and  he he says that if we carry out this  process of stripping away all these  sensible qualities and we think about  the piece of Wax  says the notion and knowledge of the wax  seems to be more clear more in that case  next in French and more distinct after  it has been discovered not only by sight  or touch but also by many other causes  with so much evidence such distinction  and clearness that I myself you know  understanding myself  can be compared to that  so our ideas already in meditation - can  indeed have this this quality qualities  of clarity and distinction  it doesn't mean yet at this point in the  game that they automatically are true in  the sense of referring to the reality of  the thing that they seem to signify if  we're talking about external things if  we're talking about things that pertain  to my own self and my own thinking well  then the clarity and distinction that we  have which dhikr takes to come in part  from what he calls the natural light we  attend to ideas and there's a sort of  natural light that illuminates them it  means that we can't be wrong about those  so this this criterion of clear and  distinct ideas is going to play an  absolutely central role in the entire  argument of the meditations and you can  see it playing an important role already  though less explicitly drawn out in the  things that Descartes is particularly  concerned with in meditation - which are  the human mind and its various types of  thoughts physical objects hopefully  outside of the mind though though we  don't know that yet and the apprehension  that we have of those objects both  through how they present themselves to  the senses and even more importantly how  the mind or understanding grasps them  you
 

